president trump is hospitalized at  walter reed medical center  after testing positive for the  coronavirus as new cases emerge among  his top advisors and allies  joining us once again from baltimore dr  tom engelsby director of the center for  health security  at johns hopkins university uh doctor i  always hesitate to ask about a specific  case  to a doctor who hasn't seen the patient  but  from your expertise knowing what we know  about the  the treatment that the president is  receiving and the fact that he's been  hospitalized  what does that tell you about his case  his condition  well chris i think the news that we  heard yesterday was largely  encouraging the fact that the president  is not requiring oxygen that  a number of lab tests were reported to  be normal and he was able to deliver  that video yesterday  but what we heard from the president's  chief of staff was concerning the the  fact that  he at least mr meadows said that his  vital signs were  concerning on friday that he was  requiring oxygen those are  those are worrisome things at least they  they suggest that the president does  have pulmonary involvement from covid if  those things are  correct and there were a number of tests  that i presume  were done at walter reed including a  chest x-ray  ct scan white blood cell count  inflammatory  inflammatory markers it would be useful  to be able to judge his risk going  forward to know what the results of  those tests were  but so far the news that we've heard has  been encouraging  well that that's encouraging to hear uh  but you anticipated where i want to take  this the president is a 74 year old man  who's  technically obese how long before we  have a sense whether  he's out of the woods and you know at  one point mark meadows was saying the  next 48 hours the doctors were saying  seven to ten days you know when are we  going to know whether he's okay and i  guess part of the question is  what is the likelihood being where he is  right now that he can participate  in a presidential debate in 11 days  it is always very difficult to make  judgments about individuals especially  unless you're really involved in the  care overall in this age  category people of the president's age  there was a large study  done by cdc in january to may time frame  that said that about a third of people  his age who were diagnosed with covid  would be hospitalized  and that there is a significant  mortality rate in that age group  now the president is receiving  extraordinary care and looks like he's  doing well but  it's certainly a serious disease for  people of his age and with  underlying condition we do know that  some people have symptoms that are mild  and recover  and do extraordinarily well in the first  week but there are others  that that in the five to ten day time  range can have a  relatively quick deterioration and  develop more severe symptoms and it's  very difficult to judge that  at this time again those additional test  results would help us make some judgment  about the likelihood of his disease  getting better  or where the potential for worsening in  terms of the debate  i i think um it's too soon to say oh go  ahead chris  i was going to ask you though because  this does get to the bigger issue  of the proper safety regulations the  rallies that the president held where  thousands of people were there uh  oftentimes  uh with no separation the vast majority  of them  not wearing masks and then as i  discussed  uh with steve cortez the president's  family and advisors  ignoring the requirement at the debate  that they wear masks as a public health  official  what do you think of that  yeah i think there is clear danger in  having  events where there are many people close  together without wearing masks for a  prolonged period of time especially  where loud voices are used that's we  know virus is spread by loud voice  more effectively so i really think large  gatherings like rallies  shouldn't be happening at this point for  any purpose during a pandemic which is  in the last few weeks actually been  getting worse  and i think when the commission sets  rules for  preventing transmission during debates  they should be followed we  people shouldn't be making their own  decisions they should be following the  rules that are established by the public  health experts who set up those debates  i want to pick up on a question i asked  mr cortez and that's the continuity of  government  because the president is in the hospital  as we say  his his prognosis is uncertain in the  course of this  your thoughts about pres vice president  pence the next in line to the presidency  going ahead with public rallies going  ahead with  this debate is that a sensible uh  action for him to take or does it raise  concerns on your part about the next  person in line to the presidency  yeah well my view at this point is that  the rest of the debates the vice  president's  vice presidential debate and if there  are presidential debates  should be virtual i think at this point  we've seen from the first debate that  there were untested unanticipated very  large risks that developed around the  debate and in the days afterwards  and in fact by public accounts it looks  like the vice president  had a meeting with the president on  tuesday a period of time where he would  be presumed to be contagious  and so in my mind it seems like the vice  president should be  in quarantine even if he is getting  tested on a normal  on a daily basis and so in any event i  don't think it  is a proper risk to take to have him  gathering with  with uh senator harris  in person and they could easily do this  debate virtually  as was done in the past for at least in  the case of kennedy and nixon i know we  did virtual debates  but for public health purposes i think  it would be safer not to have these  large gatherings at this point  uh given the risks of of the pandemic  i want to pick up on something you just 